<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jilin City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a> is a city in Jilin province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhua-river">Songhua River</a>. Its area was a centre of the ancient kingdom of Buyeo, and from the 17th century the city grew as a Qing shipbuilding base and garrison, giving its name to the province. It is now an industrial city known for its chemical works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jilin-city.jpg" alt="Jilin City" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H2v5o68z · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%90%89%E6%9E%97%E7%8E%84%E5%B8%9D%E8%A7%82_9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jilin City lies in central Jilin Province, in a hilly area on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhua-river">Songhua River</a>, which surrounds much of the city and gives it the nickname River City. Four mountains stand round it: North Mountain (Beishan) to the west, Long Tan Mountain to the east, Zhuque Mountain to the north and Turtle Mountain to the south. With the river they form a bagua in the Taiji pattern. Beishan is the best known of them and is home to several Buddhist temples. The climate is humid continental, with long, cold winters. The Songhua is the only river in the region that does not freeze in winter, and water vapour rising from it crystallises on willow branches as rime ice in January and February.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jilin City is one of the oldest cities in Northeast China. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>, the Ming sought to extend control throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and river fleets built here sailed several times to make local chieftains swear allegiance. After the Manchu-led Qing dynasty was established, the territory of today's Primorsky Krai was placed under Jilin's administration. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> advanced east towards the Pacific coast, the Qing government ordered a naval shipbuilding factory set up here in 1661.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a> was officially established as a fort city in 1673, when the Deputy Lieutenant-General Anzhuhu was ordered to build a castle. In 1676 the Military Governor of Ninguta was transferred to Jilin because of its more convenient location and growing military importance, and the city then developed rapidly. Its nickname comes from a line of a poem written by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> on his visit in 1682. Jilin remained important in the 18th and 19th centuries as one of the few cities beyond the Willow Palisade, with Qiqihar, Ninguta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Xinjing</a>, now Changchun, its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a>'s importance fell. By 1940 Jilin had 173,624 people and Xinjing 544,202. Soviet forces captured Jilin during the August Storm operation. It was the provincial capital of Jilin Province from the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949 until Changchun took that role in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jilin City is a prefecture-level city and the only major city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> that shares its name with its province. It co-hosted the Asian Winter Games in 2007, and winter sports such as skiing and skating are popular. Among its sights is Wulajie Old City, a walled Qing town downstream on the Songhua. It was a collecting point for local products sent to the imperial court, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> went there in 1682 to fish for sturgeon. A Meteorite Museum there displays the largest stony meteorite of a documented meteorite fall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jilin City” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buyeo appears in Chinese sources in the 2nd century BCE as a farming and herding kingdom on the Songhua river plain around modern <b>Jilin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/buyeo">Buyeo</a></li></ul></section>
